1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,725
A tailor sells cloth by the metre.

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:05,192
A painter charges by the square metre.

3
00:00:05,192 --> 00:00:07,217
A milk packet holds one litre.

4
00:00:07,617 --> 00:00:08,969
Three different things.

5
00:00:08,969 --> 00:00:12,265
But all three are built from
just one measurement: length.

6
00:00:12,715 --> 00:00:16,784
Aarav's bicycle speedometer says
fifteen kilometres per hour.

7
00:00:17,134 --> 00:00:18,976
Kilometres is a distance.

8
00:00:18,976 --> 00:00:20,342
Hours is a time.

9
00:00:20,342 --> 00:00:23,180
So why does one reading need two units?

10
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:27,736
By the end, you will see why, and
build even force from a few blocks.

11
00:00:28,386 --> 00:00:30,688
So what is a fundamental quantity?

12
00:00:31,038 --> 00:00:33,174
It is a building block we choose.

13
00:00:33,174 --> 00:00:35,641
It is not made from any other quantity.

14
00:00:36,041 --> 00:00:38,273
Base quantity is another name for it.

15
00:00:38,673 --> 00:00:40,456
The SI system picks seven.

16
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:45,025
Length, mass, time, electric current,
temperature, amount of substance,

17
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:46,548
and luminous intensity.

18
00:00:46,948 --> 00:00:50,002
A derived quantity is
built from these blocks.

19
00:00:50,002 --> 00:00:52,229
We only multiply or divide them.

20
00:00:52,629 --> 00:00:55,513
Area, volume, speed, density and force.

21
00:00:55,513 --> 00:00:57,278
All of them are derived.

22
00:00:57,928 --> 00:01:00,880
Let's watch Aarav ride
on our motion notebook.

23
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:02,873
The tape measures distance.

24
00:01:02,873 --> 00:01:05,078
That is a length, a base quantity.

25
00:01:05,478 --> 00:01:07,385
The stopwatch measures time.

26
00:01:07,385 --> 00:01:08,929
Another base quantity.

27
00:01:09,329 --> 00:01:11,302
Twenty metres in five seconds.

28
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:14,469
Divide, and the speed is
four metres per second.

29
00:01:14,869 --> 00:01:17,230
No instrument measured speed directly.

30
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:19,563
We built it from two base readings.

31
00:01:20,063 --> 00:01:21,598
Now he pedals harder.

32
00:01:21,598 --> 00:01:25,964
The speed climbs from four to six
metres per second in two seconds.

33
00:01:26,364 --> 00:01:27,362
Slow motion.

34
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:30,325
The blue arrow is velocity, and it grows.

35
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:34,286
The red arrow is acceleration,
how fast the speed changes.

36
00:01:34,686 --> 00:01:38,051
So acceleration is derived
too, one level higher.

37
00:01:38,701 --> 00:01:41,383
Now let's build force, one step at a time.

38
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:42,633
Step one.

39
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:44,959
Speed equals distance over time.

40
00:01:44,959 --> 00:01:47,088
A length divided by a time.

41
00:01:47,088 --> 00:01:48,977
Unit: metre per second.

42
00:01:49,727 --> 00:01:50,627
Step two.

43
00:01:50,618 --> 00:01:53,626
Acceleration is change in speed over time.

44
00:01:53,626 --> 00:01:56,298
A length, divided by time, twice.

45
00:01:56,298 --> 00:01:58,688
Unit: metre per second squared.

46
00:01:59,438 --> 00:02:00,479
Step three.

47
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:03,195
Force equals mass times acceleration.

48
00:02:03,195 --> 00:02:06,669
Mass, times length,
divided by time squared.

49
00:02:07,419 --> 00:02:11,100
So one newton is one kilogram
metre per second squared.

50
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,502
Three base units, joined together.

51
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:15,385
Remember this.

52
00:02:15,385 --> 00:02:20,105
Every derived quantity is built only
from base quantities, by multiplying

53
00:02:20,105 --> 00:02:21,185
and dividing.

54
00:02:21,885 --> 00:02:23,011
Side by side.

55
00:02:23,011 --> 00:02:24,504
Mass is fundamental.

56
00:02:24,504 --> 00:02:28,385
Put a bag of rice on a balance
and read five kilograms.

57
00:02:28,385 --> 00:02:30,462
One instrument, one reading.

58
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:32,412
Density is derived.

59
00:02:32,412 --> 00:02:37,208
One kilogram of water fills zero
point zero zero one cubic metres.

60
00:02:37,208 --> 00:02:40,712
Divide: one thousand
kilograms per cubic metre.

61
00:02:41,312 --> 00:02:42,416
Now you try.

62
00:02:42,416 --> 00:02:44,731
The milk packet says one litre.

63
00:02:44,731 --> 00:02:47,089
Is volume fundamental or derived?

64
00:02:47,089 --> 00:02:48,461
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:51,661 --> 00:02:52,561
Derived!

66
00:02:52,398 --> 00:02:55,821
One litre is a cube ten
centimetres on each side.

67
00:02:55,821 --> 00:02:58,103
Length times length times length.

68
00:02:58,753 --> 00:03:00,607
Now the opening makes sense.

69
00:03:00,607 --> 00:03:02,614
The tailor's metre is a length.

70
00:03:02,614 --> 00:03:05,638
The painter's square metre
is length times length.

71
00:03:06,038 --> 00:03:07,775
The litre is length cubed.

72
00:03:07,775 --> 00:03:10,187
All three come from one base quantity.

73
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:14,934
And kilometres per hour needs two
units because speed has two parents:

74
00:03:14,934 --> 00:03:16,476
a length and a time.

75
00:03:17,076 --> 00:03:19,182
But why are these seven the base ones?

76
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:21,666
They are chosen to be independent.

77
00:03:21,666 --> 00:03:24,769
None of the seven can be
made from the other six.

78
00:03:25,169 --> 00:03:29,334
You cannot build a length from mass
and time, or a mass from length and

79
00:03:29,334 --> 00:03:30,234
time.

80
00:03:29,841 --> 00:03:31,571
Each one is a fresh block.

81
00:03:32,171 --> 00:03:33,113
Your turn.

82
00:03:33,113 --> 00:03:35,443
Time equals distance over speed.

83
00:03:35,443 --> 00:03:37,729
So is time a derived quantity?

84
00:03:37,729 --> 00:03:39,116
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:42,316 --> 00:03:43,216
No!

86
00:03:42,768 --> 00:03:46,865
Time is a chosen base quantity,
and a clock reads it directly.

87
00:03:46,865 --> 00:03:50,304
Speed was built from time,
not the other way round.

88
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:52,690
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:57,470
A steel block is twenty centimetres
long, ten wide and five high.

90
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:00,208
Its mass is seven point eight kilograms.

91
00:04:00,208 --> 00:04:01,521
Find its density.

92
00:04:02,021 --> 00:04:04,589
First, change every length to metres.

93
00:04:04,589 --> 00:04:08,561
Zero point two, zero point
one, and zero point zero five.

94
00:04:09,311 --> 00:04:10,623
Multiply them.

95
00:04:10,623 --> 00:04:14,853
The volume is zero point
zero zero one cubic metres.

96
00:04:15,603 --> 00:04:18,083
Density equals mass over volume.

97
00:04:18,083 --> 00:04:22,178
Seven point eight divided
by zero point zero zero one.

98
00:04:22,928 --> 00:04:26,520
That is seven thousand eight
hundred kilograms per cubic metre.

99
00:04:27,170 --> 00:04:28,636
Now, the exam trap.

100
00:04:28,986 --> 00:04:32,536
Many students say electric
charge is a base quantity.

101
00:04:32,536 --> 00:04:34,022
It sounds so basic.

102
00:04:34,372 --> 00:04:37,397
But the SI base quantity
is electric current.

103
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:39,427
Charge is current times time.

104
00:04:39,427 --> 00:04:43,711
One ampere for one second is one
coulomb, so charge is derived.

105
00:04:44,361 --> 00:04:45,829
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:45,829 --> 00:04:50,392
As one mark questions: pick the
fundamental quantity, or write a unit in

107
00:04:50,392 --> 00:04:51,299
base units.

108
00:04:51,899 --> 00:04:53,170
Exam level now.

109
00:04:53,170 --> 00:04:57,102
A quantity Q equals mass
times speed, divided by time.

110
00:04:57,102 --> 00:04:59,719
What is Q, and what is its unit?

111
00:04:59,719 --> 00:05:01,044
Pause and solve.

112
00:05:06,444 --> 00:05:07,756
The answer is B.

113
00:05:07,756 --> 00:05:11,978
A derived quantity, with unit
kilogram metre per second squared.

114
00:05:12,478 --> 00:05:15,782
Mass times speed gives
kilogram metre per second.

115
00:05:15,782 --> 00:05:17,331
Option C stops here.

116
00:05:17,831 --> 00:05:22,624
Divide by time once more: kilogram
metre per second squared, the newton.

117
00:05:22,624 --> 00:05:27,428
D squares the metre by mistake, and
A calls a built quantity fundamental.

118
00:05:28,028 --> 00:05:29,483
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:29,483 --> 00:05:30,978
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:34,178 --> 00:05:37,015
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:37,415 --> 00:05:38,315
One.

122
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:40,391
Why is the litre not a base unit?

123
00:05:41,291 --> 00:05:42,191
Two.

124
00:05:41,857 --> 00:05:44,764
Write one newton using only base units.

125
00:05:45,664 --> 00:05:46,564
Three.

126
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:50,017
Is electric charge fundamental
or derived, and why?

127
00:05:51,117 --> 00:05:54,942
Next time: who decided
exactly how long one metre is?

128
00:05:54,942 --> 00:05:58,341
We find out in SI base
units and derived units.
